            id: 'ml-softmax-gradient',
            q: 'Derive the gradient of the cross-entropy loss with respect to the logits of a softmax. Why does the simple result matter?',
            level: 'advanced',
            kind: 'concept',
            testing: 'Whether you can do the one derivation every classifier and language model rests on, and say what it means for training.',
            answer: 'Call the logits z, the probabilities p = softmax(z), and the correct class y. The loss is L = −log p_y.\n\nWrite it out: L = −z_y + log Σ e^(z_j). The derivative of the log-sum-exp term with respect to z_k is e^(z_k) / Σ e^(z_j), which is exactly p_k. The first term gives −1 for the correct class and 0 for the others. So the gradient is p − y, with y as a one-hot vector: the prediction minus the target.\n\nThat matters for three reasons. The gradient is big when the model is confidently wrong and small when it is right, so learning never stalls on wrong answers. It is the same "error" form as linear regression, which is no accident: both come from maximum likelihood. And in code you compute the loss from the logits directly, with the log-sum-exp trick, because taking a softmax and then a log overflows or gives log(0).',
            analogy: 'A teacher marking a multiple-choice sheet. For every option she writes how much belief you put there, minus how much you should have: +0.7 on a wrong option you favoured, −0.9 on the right one you ignored. Those marks are the gradient.',
