            id: 'ml-is-it-better',
            q: 'Your new model scores 0.5% higher than the old one on the test set. Is it really better? How do you decide?',
            level: 'advanced',
            kind: 'scenario',
            testing: 'Whether you treat a metric as a noisy measurement, and know how to compare two models properly.',
            answer: 'Not yet. A test score is an estimate, so first I ask how noisy it is.\n\nAccuracy on n examples has a standard error of about √(p(1 − p)/n). With 1,000 examples at 90% accuracy that is about 1 percentage point, so a 0.5-point gain is well inside the noise. With 100,000 examples it is about 0.1 points, and the same gain is probably real.\n\nThen I compare the models properly. Both are run on the same examples, so I use a paired comparison: look only at the examples where they disagree, with McNemar\'s test, or bootstrap the difference and read off a confidence interval. I also retrain with a few random seeds, because run-to-run variation can be as large as the gain.\n\nFinally I check that the comparison is honest: the test set was not used for tuning, the gain holds on the slices that matter, and the metric matches what the product needs. If it still looks better, I confirm with an A/B test.',
