            id: 'ml-vae-elbo',
            q: 'What is the ELBO, and what does each of its terms do in a variational autoencoder?',
            level: 'advanced',
            kind: 'concept',
            testing: 'Whether you understand the objective behind latent-variable generative models, including why it is a bound and how gradients get through sampling.',
            answer: 'A VAE wants to maximise the probability of the data, log p(x). That needs an integral over every possible code z, which we cannot compute. So we bring in an encoder q(z | x) and get a lower bound we can compute: the ELBO, the evidence lower bound.\n\nELBO = E_q[log p(x | z)] − KL(q(z | x) ‖ p(z)).\n\nThe first term is reconstruction: sample a code for this input, decode it, and reward rebuilding the input well. The second term keeps each input\'s code distribution close to the prior, a standard normal. That packs the codes into one smooth cloud with no gaps, which is what lets you sample a random code and decode something sensible.\n\nThe two terms pull against each other: too little KL leaves gaps, too much makes the decoder ignore the code. And because z is sampled, we write z = μ + σ·ε with ε random noise, the reparameterisation trick, so gradients flow back into the encoder.',
            analogy: 'A library with a shelving rule. The first term says every book must be findable from its shelf position. The second says shelves must be filled evenly from the middle outwards, with no empty aisles. Then any random position you walk to has a real book nearby.',
            strong: [
                'Says why a bound is needed: the integral over z is intractable.',
                'States that the gap between log p(x) and the ELBO is KL(q(z | x) ‖ p(z | x)), so a better encoder tightens the bound.',
                'Explains the reparameterisation trick and names posterior collapse.',
            ],
            traps: [
                'Calling the KL term "just a regulariser" without saying it is what makes sampling possible.',
                'Saying the ELBO equals the log-likelihood. It is a lower bound.',
            ],
            followUps: [
                { q: 'Which inequality gives the bound?', a: 'Jensen\'s inequality: log is concave, so the log of an average is at least the average of the logs. Applying it to log E_q[p(x, z) / q(z | x)] gives the ELBO.' },
                { q: 'Why are VAE samples often blurry?', a: 'Each input maps to a cloud of codes, and the decoder must give one output that works for all of them. Under its loss, the safest output when a detail is uncertain is the average of the possibilities, and an average of sharp images is a blurry one.' },
                { q: 'What does a β-VAE change?', a: 'It multiplies the KL term by β. A larger β gives a tidier, more disentangled code space at the cost of blurrier reconstructions.' },
            ],
